</code></pre> <p>The example checks for an existing mnemonic in the <code>MNEMONIC</code> environment variable. If the variable is set, the mnemonic is loaded from it. Otherwise, a new random mnemonic is generated using 6251a85a-47d0-11ee-be56-0242ac120002:e1b52b9e008f8a1c78cc208ce3f1aa0b:&lt;__None__&gt;6251a85a-47d0-11ee-be56-0242ac120002:bb0cdbb78522e759804e3db70aefaad5:&lt;__None__&gt;.</p> <p>NEM uses the BIP39 standard, which represents mnemonics as 24 English words selected from a standardized word list. These words encode the entropy (randomness) used to create all derived private keys.</p> <p>Store your mnemonic phrase securely</p> <p>The mnemonic phrase can be used to regenerate all derived accounts and private keys. Anyone with access to it can control your accounts, and losing it means losing access permanently.</p> <p>Never share your mnemonic with anyone, and always store it in a secure location.</p>

